WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.960
Good morning, John. When I was growing up, there was a TV show called 'Captain Planet and the Planeteers'.

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:09.420
I always knew it was a bad TV show, but at least it was a good message.

00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:14.120
It taught kids that the world was in danger and that the power to save it is yours!

00:00:14.120 --> 00:00:16.080
But when I started my master's degree in environmental studies,

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:20.780
I was surprised to find a lot of ambivalence and even outright distaste for 'Captain Planet'.

00:00:20.780 --> 00:00:22.780
That's weird, so I wanted to talk about it.

00:00:22.780 --> 00:00:24.040
First, though, a synopsis:

00:00:24.040 --> 00:00:28.580
Gaia realizes she's been napping for too long and that humans are at it again.

00:00:28.580 --> 00:00:30.540
[Gaia] "It's those poor, silly humans again."

00:00:30.540 --> 00:00:34.400
So she gives some rings to five teens and they get special powers and can work together...

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:37.000
...to call a blue guy with a mullet and bad puns.

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:39.260
And I like puns; these ones are just bad.

00:00:39.260 --> 00:00:41.640
[Captain Planet] "Your evil plans are on the rocks!"

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:46.860
The idea is that it's half fun adventure and half "here's what you can do to lessen your impact on the environment".

00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:48.480
[character] Wash only full loads of laundry!

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:52.520
And that's how, for the first season at least, it was sold to a ridiculously star-studded cast:

00:00:52.960 --> 00:00:53.840
Whoopi Goldberg,

00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:54.640
Meg Ryan,

00:00:54.640 --> 00:00:55.440
LeVar Burton,

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:56.020
Sting,

00:00:56.160 --> 00:01:01.380
and this greedy, big-nosed rat-looking guy poisoning the water supply played by  - oh my god - Jeff Goldblum, why?

00:01:01.380 --> 00:01:04.000
[Captain Planet] It's either the police station or the zoo!

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:06.000
[Jeff Goldblum] The zoo! The zoo. That sounds good!

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:12.020
The entire project was conceived and funded by Ted Turner, who also owned the TV stations that it ran on.

00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:15.960
So why do I --  a definite environmentalist -- have a problem with this?

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:17.350
[teen] Captain Planet's in trouble!

00:01:17.350 --> 00:01:19.739
Well, kids shows have to have villains,

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:23.620
But when that dynamic is being applied to real world...

00:01:23.660 --> 00:01:29.820
...discussions and issues, that villain becomes a straw man for someone real. They're caricatures.

00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:34.460
Subhuman evildoers destroy the environment for...fun?

00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:41.939
Almost all of them with some kind of physical deformity. And many coded to be lower-class for some reason? Most of these villains work in...

00:01:42.220 --> 00:01:48.299
...extractive industries: timber, mining, oil, energy, and we never see any examples of this work being done responsibly.

00:01:48.759 --> 00:01:52.679
One of the villains is a garbage collector, like why is that bad?

00:01:52.680 --> 00:01:58.620
The lesson seems to be that this work can't be done sustainably, and the people who do it, do it because they're monsters.

00:01:58.760 --> 00:02:03.360
[Villain] If there's anything I like more than being mean, it's being sneaky.

00:02:03.700 --> 00:02:08.420
That isn't fun for the people or families who depend on that work, and it's also not

00:02:08.500 --> 00:02:09.000
honest.

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:14.760
It might to some look like a bunch of rich people living in big buildings made out of mined minerals with lives powered by fossil fuels,

00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:21.560
portraying the people who provided those luxuries as evil. Like, Ted Turner has a nonzero number of private jets.

00:02:21.770 --> 00:02:27.520
We don't rip trees out of the ground for fun. We do it to build homes and make paper. We don't drill for oil...

00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:31.929
...so we can dump it on the ground, we drill for oil because people buy it so that they can get to work...

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:37.060
...and to the grocery store. The people who work in extractive industries are real people, not pigmen.

00:02:37.080 --> 00:02:40.260
[Villain] I'll be able to drill for oil anywhere!

00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:44.760
And environmentalists have to work with those real people when trying to get policies passed.

00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:49.960
They become their friends, or at least their colleagues, and seeing examples of being literally...

00:02:50.020 --> 00:02:54.840
...dehumanized by the movement that now suddenly wants to have a good faith discussion isn't helpful.

00:02:54.850 --> 00:02:56.850
I'm not saying that Captain Planet did no good,

00:02:57.140 --> 00:03:03.250
people did learn good behaviors, and it created a group of young people who loudly supported environmental regulation and sustainable energy;

00:03:03.500 --> 00:03:09.790
But a lot of that energy came from vilifying a lot of people who had to swallow their pride and come to the negotiating table...

00:03:10.160 --> 00:03:15.519
...despite being portrayed as monsters because they helped provide the energy and resources that we all use.

00:03:15.519 --> 00:03:18.999
I know that children's television is not the place to go for political nuance,

00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:23.100
but maybe that's a good reason to not have children's shows about politics.

00:03:23.100 --> 00:03:26.500
If it was only about respect or love or appreciation for the environment,

00:03:26.500 --> 00:03:27.860
that would be fine, but it's not.

00:03:27.860 --> 00:03:32.840
And while some of the after-episode mini-lessons provide nuance, the stories rarely do.

00:03:32.840 --> 00:03:36.220
It looks to me like an intentional formation of a rift,

00:03:36.440 --> 00:03:45.000
teaching children that the problem is bad humans, not hard problems, and that when people disagree it's because they are monsters who love to destroy beauty.

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:47.840
It looks a little like a microcosm for all politics these days.

00:03:48.470 --> 00:03:51.459
Captain Planet helped us win some good policies, but in the process...

00:03:51.459 --> 00:03:57.429
...it deepened a rift. We did that, and I think it's worth thinking about. John, I'll see you on Tuesday.

